.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_CREATE_MP 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_create_mp \- create a persistent event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop ccol_event_loop_create_mp(size_t max_events_per_wait,
                        size_t num_lock_stripes, size_t num_reactor_threads,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a persistent kernel event queue (an epoll(7) instance on
Linux, a kqueue(2) on FreeBSD) and starts the thread or threads that
drive it at once. ccol_select() builds a fresh epoll instance on
every call and tears it down once a selectable is ready, but the epoll
instance and the threads of a ccol_event_loop live as long as the
handle does, and ccol_event_loop_add, ccol_event_loop_modify and
ccol_event_loop_remove change its registrations one at a time.

Exactly ONE dedicated thread calls epoll_wait, whatever the value of
num_reactor_threads; there is never a second one. With
num_reactor_threads == 1 that thread polls and also runs every callback
inline. A larger value starts the same single poller thread plus
(num_reactor_threads - 1) separate dispatch worker threads that run the
callbacks. Either way the loop uses exactly num_reactor_threads OS
threads in total, and all of them are ready to work when this call
returns, the same way a ctpool_create() pool is ready the moment you
get the handle.

With num_reactor_threads > 1 the loop enforces two correctness
properties that a single thread gets for free, since it is the only
caller. The callbacks of one registration never run at the same time
as each other, and a read registration and a write registration on the
same fd never run at the same time either. The second rule goes further
than "no self-concurrency": two callbacks that share state across both
directions of one fd (one TLS connection object, for example) need no
lock of their own.

The loop never collects a second dispatch for a registration while an
earlier one is queued or running, so application code can call
ccol_event_loop_modify from inside a callback that is in flight. That
is a supported and common pattern, and it never races with a second
dispatch of the same registration collected at the same moment. The
same design also gives the caller an identity token, described in
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation(3).

A registration is built from the same ccol_selectable type that
ccol_select uses, with the builders ccol_selectable_from_fd,
ccol_selectable_from_circq, ccol_selectable_from_dynq and
ccol_selectable_from_chan. Like ccol_select, ccol_event_loop never does
the receive or the send itself for any type of selectable; the callback
always does it. ccol_event_loop_add(3) explains why.

Inside the library each registered fd is backed by one persistent entry
in the epoll interest list. Both directions share that entry, because
epoll_ctl keys its interest list by fd and not by an (fd, direction)
pair. Each queue or ccol_channel selectable gets its own bridge
eventfd(2), which stays linked into the queue's waiter list for good,
unlike the temporary waiter nodes that ccol_select() makes on each
call.

The registry of fds and registrations is lock-striped. It holds
num_lock_stripes independent (mutex, chmap) pairs, and each pair guards
its own subset of the registrations. A real fd is handled by exactly
one stripe for its whole lifetime, and so is the private bridge eventfd
of a queue or ccol_channel registration, so no operation ever needs the
lock of more than one stripe. A value of 1 uses a single lock (one
mutex and one chmap, plus one more array indirection). A larger value
lets ccol_event_loop_add, ccol_event_loop_remove and
ccol_event_loop_modify calls for different fds or registrations run in
parallel instead of queuing up behind one lock, at the cost of
num_lock_stripes mutexes and chmaps allocated up front.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_events_per_wait  Size of the poller thread's event batch buffer,
                      from 1 to INT_MAX inclusive, and small enough
                      that max_events_per_wait times the size of one
                      kernel event record (struct epoll_event on
                      Linux, struct kevent on FreeBSD) fits in a
                      size_t. The value is passed as the int event
                      count of epoll_wait(2) or kevent(2), and the
                      poller's events buffer is sized from it.
num_lock_stripes     Number of independent lock stripes for the
                      registry of fds and entries, at least 1 and
                      with no upper bound. With 1 there is a single
                      shared lock.
num_reactor_threads  Total number of OS threads the loop uses for
                      polling and dispatching, at least 1. With 1, a
                      single thread polls and dispatches inline; any
                      larger value gives exactly one dedicated poller
                      thread plus (num_reactor_threads - 1) dispatch
                      worker threads.
mmgmt_procs          Custom memory management procedures, or NULL for
                      the default malloc and free.
err_str               Optional pointer that gets an error string on a
                      failure. Pass NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new ccol_event_loop handle on success.

CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_NULL on failure. The causes are: max_events_per_wait
is 0, larger than INT_MAX, or overflows size_t when multiplied by the
size of one kernel event record; num_lock_stripes is 0;
num_reactor_threads is 0; mmgmt_procs is malformed because it sets some
but not all of malloc, calloc, realloc and free; an allocation failed;
epoll_create1(2) (kqueue(2) on FreeBSD), eventfd(2) or
pthread_create(3) failed; the process had no thread-specific key left
for the two keys that every loop's dispatch uses (they are created once
per process and never retried); the library's process-exit destructors
have run and the last loop is gone, for example in an application
destructor that runs after them; or no handle slot could be allocated.
In that last case the poller thread, and the dispatch pool if the
configuration made one, are stopped again before the call returns.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The handle is an opaque VALUE and not a pointer. Do not cast it to or
from void*, and do not use it as an address. Compare it with
CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_NULL; that value is 0, so a test such as if (!loop)
is correct as well. Every call resolves the handle before it touches
the loop, so a stale or destroyed handle is refused and never
dereferenced.

This page names the epoll(7) calls and flags of the Linux build. On
FreeBSD the same reactor runs on kqueue(2) and behaves the same way:
level-triggered reporting, one-shot re-arming when a dispatch pool
exists, and the same delivery of errors and hang-ups.

A process can hold several independent ccol_event_loop instances. Each
has its own epoll instance, its own reactor threads and its own array
of lock stripes, and shares no global state with the others, so
num_lock_stripes and num_reactor_threads really are per-instance
settings.

Only one thread ever blocks in epoll_wait, whatever num_reactor_threads
is. If several threads called epoll_wait on the same instance, one
ready event could wake all of them, which is a thundering herd inside
the kernel. EPOLLEXCLUSIVE doesn't help, because it applies to one fd
registered in several SEPARATE epoll instances, not to many threads
sharing one instance. Such wake-ups add dispatch latency even to a
single connection that nothing else competes for.

fork(2) and ccol_event_loop can be combined in two supported ways:
fork(2) before you create a loop, or create one and then call fork(2)
followed at once by exec(3). A process that keeps running with no
exec(3) after the fork, while a loop it created before the fork is
live, is not supported.

As a defense in depth beyond that rule, a fresh call from any thread
(ccol_event_loop_create_mp, _add, _remove, _modify, _pause, _resume and
_destroy) never hangs on a lock, in the parent or in a freshly forked
child, because some other thread held that lock at the instant of the
fork. That thread may not exist in the child, since fork(2) duplicates
only the calling thread.

This does nothing for a loop that is already live across the fork. The
child gets no copy of its poller and dispatch threads, so an inherited
loop cannot dispatch anything there. Treat it as inert in the child:
calling ccol_event_loop_destroy on it is safe, but it can't be used for
any more dispatch.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call while other threads work on unrelated ccol_event_loop
instances. There is no handle yet for any other call to race with.
.fi
